      "predicted_answer": "They perform joint training by transforming BERT into a shared task representation encoder (STR-encoder) using a dynamic range attention mechanism. The STR-encoder contains an embedding layer and N multi-head self-attention layers divided into two blocks: the first N-K layers capture context information (shared across tasks), while the last K layers use task-specific MASK matrices (MASK^ner for NER, MASK^rc for RC) to focus attention on task-relevant words. During joint training, the model takes the [CLS] token and entity markers as input to BERT, obtains the STR-encoder output H_N, then feeds H_N into separate task-specific output layers for NER (token-level classification with BIOES tagging) and RC (relation classification using [CLS] and entity marker representations). The NER and RC losses are summed (L = L_ner + L_rc) and backpropagated jointly through the shared STR-encoder, allowing the shared layers to learn joint features between entity and relation types. Ablation shows joint training improves NER F1 by 0.52% and RC F1 by 2.37% over separate training.",
